@@ -58,6 +58,30 @@ It throws a `JsonPatchError` (whose `index` property is the position of the oper
   into one of the value's own children (a `move` onto its own location changes nothing, but its `from`
   must exist), a `test` whose value differs (objects compare regardless of member order), or a `remove` of the whole document. A pointer error is kept as the error's `cause`.
 
+### JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch, JsonMergePatchError } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyMergePatch(
+  { title: "Goodbye!", author: { givenName: "John", familyName: "Doe" }, tags: ["example", "sample"] },
+  { title: "Hello!", author: { familyName: null }, tags: ["example"] },
+); // { title: "Hello!", author: { givenName: "John" }, tags: ["example"] }
+```
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` returns a new document: an object `patch` merges into `target` member by
+member, recursively, where a `null` member removes the target's member (if any) and any other member
+replaces or merges into it; a `patch` that is not an object, an array included, replaces the whole
+target, and an object `patch` applied to a non-object target starts from `{}`. Arrays are never merged,
+and a `null` inside an array is kept. The algorithm is that of RFC 7386, section 2, unchanged by
+RFC 7396 which obsoletes it.
+
+`target` and `patch` must be JSON values, as for `applyPatch`; neither is modified, and the result shares
+no object or array with them. Only own members are merged into, and a `__proto__` member is an ordinary
+member. Every JSON value is a valid merge patch, so the only error is a `JsonMergePatchError` thrown when
+`target` or `patch` is not a JSON value. A merge patch cannot set a member to `null` nor change part of
+an array: use JSON Patch for that.
+
 ## Development
 
 `npm test` runs the tests with Node's test runner (Node 22.18 or later, which runs TypeScript directly).
